 and even though my  kid like is a really good sleeper he's  by 4 months he was sleeping 7 to 7 which  is amazing you know I would be up twice  in the night to  and that could take me like 30 minutes  40 minutes and then it would take me  another like hour so to wind back down  so that was a total struggle and by the  time I quit pumping in the middle of the  night it was like the world opened up to  me and I was like this is what sleep  feels like and I had an amazing stretch  of sleep for like a good you know like  six to nine months maybe and then in the  spring my son turned one and literally  hit the fan I started thinking about my  infertility again which I don't know  maybe I'll I'll talk to you guys about  that someday when I'm ready when I feel  up to it but I had some you know major  business restructuring some massive  friend drama and on top of all that work  really was starting to ramp right up so  my channel is growing and obviously that  meant you know lots of love but ultimate  lots hate and trolls and I'm no stranger  to trolls it doesn't really bother me  but it was just a lot to try to juggle  and manage and respond to at the same  time and I don't know if everyone knows  this but my YouTube channel is just one  part of my business I have a lot of  other things going on so needless to say  the year was super stressful and  exhausting and ultimately really hard  and it really impacted my sleep now  before I go on I want to just preface  that I have never actually taken any  prescription sleeping pills it's not  that I think that they're bad or I want  anyone to think that they shouldn't feel  guilty or ashamed for taking them but  just noting that I have felt like I  needed or wanted to do so in the past at  the recognition of a sleep psychiatrist  I had seen I kind of allowed myself to  take something 3 days of the week or  three nights of the week something over  the counter like zzzquil or gravel or  melt like high-dose melatonin something  like that and that was working for me  really well at the start when I was  having a stressful day I would be like  okay this is the night that I need to  take something to just let me relax and  get a good sleep but as my stress was  mount  and the kind of anxiety was taking over  I was finding it really hard to cope  without taking something so usually at  some point in the night I would kind of  panic because I hadn't been sleeping for  a few hours and I would like even if it  wasn't one of those three days that I  had allotted to taking like a gravel or  melatonin I would feel like I needed to  have something and take something and  because I was always left I was already  like a few hours into this process of  trying to get to sleep I would be so  riled up that the drug wouldn't even  work so then I would feel like I need to  take something else on top of that or  more a higher dose and sometimes I would  eventually fall asleep after a few hours  and sometimes I wouldn't sleep at all  and those were the rock-bottom days some  nights I should say those were the worst  and you know eventually got so bad that  I was feeling like the drugs just  weren't helping me anymore and that's  what happens when you use a lot of these  sleeping aids they may not be addictive  but they're kind of psychologically  addicting and not like you you're  dependent on them and the same dose does  not really work so well anymore you need  higher doses to get the same effect so  that kind of freaked me out and that's  when I decided I wanted to get off like  all of these medications and employ a  really strict sleep hygiene protocol in  its place okay so what did that look  like well first of all of course I'd  have dinner six o'clock  have dinner with my family put my kid to  bed around 7:00 then I'd watch my face  get ready for bed avoid any liquid  anywhere close to bedtime and only decaf  during the day I would get into bed  really early like maybe like 7:30 years  or so and I start with a meditation of  mindfulness meditation I like  progressive muscle relaxation that's one  of my favorites you can find those on  YouTube or just kind of download an app  for them and then I put white noise I've  got a white noise app on my phone put  that on my phone away wouldn't look at  my phone so no blue light emitted no  screen time at all and then I would do  some journaling so I had a journal kept  it by my bed I would write down anything  that was you know stressing me out or  anything I was thinking about in the day  that might come up or creep up at night  and then I would get out like a really  boring book and I would start to read  until my eyes started to get like really  tired and droopy basically and then I  had to attempt to go to sleep at like  8:00 p.m. I know like geriatric hour  around here so like no TV or anything  like that I also would get out of bed  anytime that I wasn't sleeping for like  more than 20 minutes I'd get out of bed  which sucks and I'd go read in my office  or I go to the guest room and read there  I also would not nap in the day that's  another really common recommendation  from sleep experts no napping even if  you're exhausted would never attempt a  nap so my bedtime just kept getting like  earlier and earlier because I just was  so exhausted from just sleeping so  poorly and also if I like did finally  fall asleep I'd be waking up at like 3  or 2 in the morning and staying up so I  genuinely was tired when I did hit the  sheets but I was just so fearful of  pushing my bed time back any further  because I was afraid that it would mean  even less sleep so I literally had like  no social life because I was just so  chained to my perfect sleep routine well  guess what not only did this not work  but I actually think that it was making  it way way worse because this perfect  sleep routine was becoming kind of  psychologically associated with me not  sleeping I also used like a bunch of  different meditation and story apps like  sleeping apps like sleepy sleep with me  calm headspace like I tried everything  but the problem was that basically what  happened is I would memorize them and as  it got to the end of the story or the  end of the meditation I would start  freaking out in my head being like oh my  gosh we're near the end 45 minutes has  gone by and I'm still not asleep  it's like I would hear the jingle of  like the start of a new a new story or  the start of a new kind of episode and I  would like send me into panic mode I  don't know if anyone else can can relate  to that but it is like  worst problem to have so yeah that  totally made it worse now the next thing  we tried was hypnosis I know that  hypnosis sounds like super worried and  like total the thing is I think it just  misunderstood because it's it's not like  magic it's actually just almost like a  personalized meditation recording but of  course the same thing would happen that  was happening to me with all of my  meditation apps and that was that I was  just memorizing the recording and as we  got to the end it was causing me even  more anxiety so it obviously did not  work either so next on my attempt list  was CBT I so I CBT I stands for  cognitive behavioral therapy for  insomnia and I've done CBT for general  anxiety for years and if you want me to  speak about my anxiety journey if that's  when you're in student and definitely  leave me a comment below and I will get  to that in a whole other video that's a  totally different beast so basically  this is the process where your therapist  will restrict the amount of time that  you spend in bed so severely that  ultimately you become so exhausted you  just can't help but sleep so in this  scenario my usual bedtime of like a PM  was being shifted to 11:00 p.m. I wasn't  allowed to like go to sleep or fall  asleep till 11:00 and then no matter  what I'd have to wake up at 4:00 a.m. so  what that meant was that even if I toss  and turn and couldn't fall asleep till  like 2:00 a.m. which was not uncommon it  meant that my alarm was gonna go off at  4:00 a.m. too bad so sad I did not like  that idea just the thought of having to  stay awake without even the possibility  of sleeping in or going to bed a little  earlier the next night to make up for  like a really poor sleep the night  before it made me so anxious that like I  couldn't sleep at all I would just be  like so fearful of bedtime all day long  it just it didn't work for me now I know  that CBT I does work I mean it's like  the gold standard it has a really high  success rate for insomnia but I think  you need to have some kind of  flexibility in your work situation to be  a little bit of a zombie while you go  through the process and I just didn't I  didn't  I just didn't right now I've been so  busy I needed to feel like I would have  some energy to function in the day what  I did take out of cbt-i which i think is  really useful and helpful for everyone  is that I kind of poisoned my bed and  what that means is that I was  associating my bed with not sleeping so  it meant I had to be super strict with  myself about getting out of bed if I was  not sleeping there so if I was tossing  and turning for like 20 minutes or more  had to get out and do something else  otherwise the moment I would get into  bed it would be like ding all of the  insomnia thoughts would come rushing  back I'm also taking some supplements  some kind of natural sleeping  supplements one is magnesium comm powder  it's like calm it's like a powder that  you take before bed that's been awesome  I will note however if you're gonna try  that that you want to start on like a  low dose and work your way up because it  can cause I don't think diarrhea but can  cause like loose bowels so the speaker  some kind of digestive issues so just  don't go crazy on the portion sizes for  that the other one is rhodiola which is  a and adaptogenic herb which honestly  I've always thought was totally bullsh  but even it's the Plitt if it's the  placebo effect at this point I was kind  of like willing to try anything and then  the other thing is a time-released  melatonin and I used to take like 10  milligrams of melatonin plus a sleeping  aid now I'm down to like just a few  milligrams of time-release melatonin and  I'm hoping to be off it completely by  the end of the year so that's been  really helpful just giving myself  permission to do this out of slowly at a  slower pace and so note that I do really  think that the time-release formulas are  way better than just the the one use  formulas for melatonin because I'm the  kind of sleeper that wakes up in the  middle of the night and sometimes has a  really hard time getting back to sleep  so that's why I like the time release  versions second the other thing that was  really important for me was I had to  prove to myself that I could in fact  operate on very little sleep so a couple  weeks ago  I did not sleep there's one night that I  slept maybe one or two hours like  nothing and that was because the next  day I had a really long packed shoot day  which is really physically and like  mentally exhausting and demanding on me  and I often have a lot of anxiety that I  just won't have the stamina to get  through it so the fear of not being able  to like perform spiralled me into these  anxious nighttime thoughts and they  become a self-fulfilling prophecy of  course and then you don't sleep and my  therapist told me you know Abby your  pulleys gonna be okay and I was I made  it through the shoot day I was fine it  sucked and I need to always remind  myself now that yeah like not sleeping  at night it's not fun and it does suck  and the day after is gonna suck but I'm  a mother super woman and I can make it  through I can still get through the day  and then I can go home and I can crash  and I can get back on track the third  thing that I did was I started to take  intermittent breaks from social media  and I deleted all my social media apps  on my phone this was really hard for me  to do because like a lot of you guys  probably I am like totally addicted to  my phone and taking those apps off my  phone really made it abundantly clear  that it's a bit of a problem but I can  only say that this alone probably made  the biggest immediate obvious difference  for me and what I really just come to  realize is that it's just not always so  healthy for me to be constantly checking  up and costly kind of scrolling and  constantly looking at my social media  feeds every hour of the day so that  really really made a difference for me  in my sleep and the last thing that I  did that really helped with my sleeping  was I had to stop with the really strict  sleeping regimes and routines all of  which of course were designed to help  improve my sleep hygiene and help me  sleep better but they were actually  doing the opposite so I felt that I  really had to loosen the reins a bit so  one thing I've been doing is I've been  watching TV in my den with my husband  and I used to never allow myself to  watch TV because again screen time but  one thing I like to do now is I try to  switch up the shows that I watch because  I was finding that I would  memorize the theme song and then when I  hear that theme song come on again even  if my eyes are closer I'm kind of dozing  out I would just kind of like get into  panic mode like oh my god another  episodes gone by I'm not asleep etc so  longer shows our best and kind of boring  shows things that don't require a ton of  concentration so really light-hearted  stuff and I was just finding that  usually I would just fall asleep on the  couch and then I would go upstairs and  and get to bed when I was already in  that like total drowsy state another fun  thing that we've been doing is my  husband and I got like a really long  boring  thousand-piece puzzle oh man puzzles are  so hard so they they're so boring and  sometimes in the middle of the night if  I'm not sleeping that's another thing  that I'll get doing but puzzles are a  great way to kind of just unwind after a  really long day and then some nights  when I'm really already quite tired I  skip the TV downstairs and I skip the  the puzzles or the books or whatever and  I like already just go right into bed  and I'll sometimes just like watch a  little show with my husband on occasion  that happens like maybe once a week and  that is so against the sleep hygiene  rules but I have found that loosening  the rules and being not so rigid in my  sleep routine has actually become the  best way to actually get good sleep  there are definitely days that I still  don't sleep well particularly when you  know I've got something really stressful  going on at work or if I really  overthink the routine but I will say in  general I am doing so much better just  by chilling out a little bit about sleep  I had a therapist once say to me that  the more work we put into trying to  sleep  the harder is to sleep so in other words  sleeping ultimately you should be  thoughtless like it should be effortless  so the same way I talk about intuitive  eating I'm kind of trying to think about  sleep the same way right like sleep and  food and eating are both actually quite  intuitive we're born just knowing and  and wanting these things but it's when  we start to overthink them and  manipulate them that's when they become  over complicated and ultimately that  process gets disturbed so I'm just  trying to think in  those similar terms and put the same  kind of thought process and philosophy  into my sleep that I did employ with my  eating for intuitive eating so guys I  hope you enjoyed this vlog if you did be  sure to give it the thumbs up leave me a  comment below if there's anything else  you guys want me to talk about in my  personal life but also any topics  related to health nutrition etc do you  like these blog videos is there anything  else you'd like to see me do subscribe  to the channel 